<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Road Obstacle Video Segmentation</span></p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">(Supplementary Material)</span></p>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the following sections, we present additional analysis (<a href="#S7" title="7 Additional Analysis ‣ Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), along with extended dataset details in <a href="#S8" title="8 Additional Dataset Details ‣ Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13181v1/cvpr_supp_3.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/381;" width="429" height="381" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Predicted road-obstacle maps obtained from the baselines (RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>) and our proposed method CC-SAM2 on the LidarSOD dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13181v1/cvpr_sup_img_4.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/102;" width="471" height="102"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average road-obstacles pixel distribution in different datasets. This illustrates the average location of the obstacles on the road.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13181v1/cvpr_supp_img_5.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/132;" width="471" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Shows the frequency of road obstacles having varying sizes. Inside the red bounding boxes, it shows that the majority of road obstacles are small in size.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13181v1/supfig.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/284;" width="471" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We show that while curating Apolloscape-Road-Obstacle, the intersection of road segment with the obstacle centroids help removing the obstacles that are outside the road.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Additional Analysis</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Domain shift Effect</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss the effect of the domain shift in the road-obstacle video segmentation performance. The LidarSOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> dataset is collected from an Indian road scene scenario, which is significantly different from the training dataset Cityscapes-VPS in terms of traffic scenarios and scene layout, since the latter is captured in Germany. As a result, we qualitatively observe in <a href="#S6.F5" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, that all methods predict many false positives. In addition, <a href="#S6.F5" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> first column shows the input image captured during the night, where we can observe that under such poor illumination conditions the false positives are increased further. Thus, making road-obstacle segmentation more challenging.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Qualitative Examples</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide further qualitative examples in Figs. <a href="#S8.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 8 Additional Dataset Details ‣ Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S8.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 8 Additional Dataset Details ‣ Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Compared to prior image-based methods (<span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>), we observe that CC-SAM2 provides far more accurate segmentation maps, further underscoring the benefits of utilizing video foundation models for our task. We also notice that the masks generated by CC-SAM2 are more accurate than those of HM2F-Video.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Additional Dataset Details</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold on road-obstacle size: <a href="#S6.F7" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_medium"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></span> illustrates the distribution of road obstacles of various sizes across different datasets. Notably, very small road obstacles appear frequently in all datasets. To address this, we applied a size threshold, removing obstacles smaller than 100 pixels in LidarSOD and 225 pixels in both SOS and Lost&amp;Found. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average road-obstacle pixel distribution: </span>
In <a href="#S6.F6" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we present the average pixel distribution of road obstacles across different datasets. In Lost&amp;Found <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and SOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, obstacles are primarily located in the middle of the road, whereas in LidarSOD, they tend to appear along the road’s periphery.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Apolloscape-Road-Obstacle dataset curation details:</span> While curating the AsRO dataset, some obstacles were not present on the road, but had an overlap with some road pixels, as shown in <a href="#S6.F8" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> (inside zoomed box). Therefore, we must remove such obstacles, since they are not on the road. We first calculate the convex hull of all obstacle instances. Then, for each obstacle instance, we calculate the intersection of the road segment with the obstacle centroid. Following the aforementioned steps, we remove such road obstacles as shown in <a href="#S6.F8" title="In Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13181v1/Cvpr_SUP_Img_1.png" id="S8.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/338;" width="471" height="338" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S8.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Predicted road-obstacle maps obtained from the baselines (RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>) and our proposed method CC-SAM2 and HM2F-Video on the Lost&amp;Found dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S8.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13181v1/Cvpr_SUP_Img_2.png" id="S8.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/338;" width="471" height="338" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S8.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Predicted road-obstacle maps obtained from the baselines (RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>) and our proposed method CC-SAM2 and HM2F-Video on the SOS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>: </span> The SOS dataset comprises 20 video sequences captured in real world road environments. Each sequence is recorded at 25 FPS, with every eighth frame annotated. This results in a total of 1,129 pixel-level semantically-annotated images. The obstacles present in the dataset follow the same labeling method as the Cityscapes dataset. It includes 13 types of obstacles present in the road scenes, such as bags and balls. Additionally, the SOS dataset contains two classes: road-obstacle/out-of-distribution (OOD) and Road.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All other areas apart from OOD and road are considered void.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LidarSOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>:</span> comprises 15 video sequences. The total number of annotated images is 2919. The ground truth maps have three classes: road, off-road (buildings, cars, pedestrians etc.), and obstacle (with nine types of classes like stones, bricks, dogs etc.). 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S8.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lost&amp;Found <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>:</span> consists of 13 different street scene sequences with 37 different types of obstacles that are present on the road. The dataset provides coarse annotations of free-space areas and pixel-level fine-grained annotations of obstacles present on the road. The dataset has a total of 2239 annotated frames.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All the baseline models are based on the mask-transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> architecture. During inference, the model outputs a set of <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">class masks</span> <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> along with their corresponding <span id="S9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">class scores</span> <math id="S9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, which are used to compute anomaly scores. In our work, we establish two baseline categories:(a) Image-based obstacle segmentation methods, and (b) Video-based semantic segmentation methods. We provide a detailed implementation discussion of each category and corresponding methods in the following sub-sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.1 </span>Image-based obstacle segmentation methods</h3>

<div id="S9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All the image obstacle segmentation methods are based on Mask2Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which consists of an image encoder, a pixel decoder, and a transformer decoder. We used Swin-B as the image encoder, and its weights are initialized from pre-trained ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Next, we utilized a multi-scale deformable attention transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> as the pixel decoder, giving feature maps at <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/8,1/16," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/8,1/16,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/32</annotation></semantics></math> resolution to provide image features to the transformer decoder layers. The transformer decoder consists of 9 layers (except for RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> where the number of decoder layers is 1) with 100 queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The training details for the image-based obstacle segmentation are mainly derived from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. We train each of the methods on the Cityscapes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> dataset, with an initial learning rate of 1e-4 and batch size of 16 for 90 thousand iterations on AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> with a weight decay of 0.05. We now describe how to obtain anomaly scores for each image-based obstacle segmentation method.</p>
<ul id="S9.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximum softmax</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> is the most commonly used obstacle segmentation method. Given an image <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, the anomaly score <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{MSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{MSP}</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated as:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{MSP}(I)=1-\max(C^{T}\times M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{MSP}(I)=1-\max(C^{T}\times M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Energy</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> detect obstacle based on the difference of energies between in-distribution (known classes) and out-of-distribution (obstacles). We calculate anomaly score for an image <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{Energy}(I)=-logsumexp(C^{T}\times M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{Energy}(I)=-logsumexp(C^{T}\times M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> in anomaly segmentation measures the uncertainty. With higher entropy indicating anomaly or road obstacle region. Now, for an image <math id="S9.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, we can calculate the anomaly score as:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{Entropy}(I)=-p\text{log}p,\&gt;\&gt;p=(C^{T}\times M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>log</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.607em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{Entropy}(I)=-p\text{log}p,\&gt;\&gt;p=(C^{T}\times M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Max Logit</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> have shown to greatly outperforms the maximum softmax. Max logit can be calculated as negative of the maximum unnormalized logit to get anomaly score that can be equated as:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{MaxLogit}(I)=-\max(C^{T}\times M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{MaxLogit}(I)=-\max(C^{T}\times M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Void Classifier</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> utilizes the background class as anomaly or road obstacle class. The anomaly score can be yielded as:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{VoidClassifier}(I)=(C^{T}\times M)_{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{VoidClassifier}(I)=(C^{T}\times M)_{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.I1.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> here represents the score obtained from the void class.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining image obstacle segmentation methods: Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, EAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, AEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> utilize an additional fine-tuning step called outlier exposure, where synthetic anomalies are utilized to further improve the road-obstacle segmentation performance. Now, we will discuss each of the method in more detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S9.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S9.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mask2Anomaly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite></span> during outlier exposure train the model using mask-contrastive loss. The synthetic outlier image is generated using AnomalyMix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> where an object from MS-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> dataset image is cut and pasted on the Cityscapes image. The network is trained for 4000 iterations with a learning rate of 1e-5, and batch size 8, keeping all the other hyper-parameters the same. The probability of choosing an outlier image in a training batch is kept at 0.2. During inference the anomaly score is calculated using maximum softmax.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RbA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite></span> performs outlier exposure by sampling 300 images from MS-COCO. The model is trained for 2K iterations with a batch size of 16. The probability of choosing an outlier image in a training batch is set to 0.2. To calculate anomaly score during inference, it employs the following equation:</p>
<table id="S9.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{RbA}(I)=-\sum\gamma(C^{T}\times M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{RbA}(I)=-\sum\gamma(C^{T}\times M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tanh</annotation></semantics></math> function.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and AEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite></span> fine-tune its model for 2K iterations during the outlier exposure step utilizing the ADE20K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> to generate the outlier images. AEM during anomaly inference use max logit to get anomaly scores whereas EAM employs the following equation to detect anomalous region:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{EAM}(I)=-(C^{T}\times\max(M))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{EAM}(I)=-(C^{T}\times\max(M))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2 </span>Video-based semantic segmentation methods</h3>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since no video road obstacle segmentation method previously existed, we repurposed existing video-based semantic segmentation approaches: M2F-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and DVIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Both methods are based on Mask2Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which we adapted for our task by applying the Max Softmax technique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> to get anomaly scores. We train both methods on the Cityscapes-VPS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. In the rest of the section, we provide a detailed implementation detail of each approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S9.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S9.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M2F-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite></span> is trained using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.0001 and a weight decay of 0.05. The training is conducted for 16 thousand iterations with a batch size of 4. During training, we sample two consecutive frames from each video clip and resize them to 512<math id="S9.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>256 pixels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DVIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite></span> comprises of M2F-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and a temporal refiner consisting of six temporal decoder blocks. It is trained using the AdamW optimizer with a base learning rate of 1e-4 and a weight decay of 5e-2. The training is performed for 16,000 iterations with a batch size of 4.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Zero-shot Road-Obstacle Video Segmentation</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAM 2 gives class-agnostic outputs that limits its utility for road-obstacle segmentation task. To address this, we use CLIP’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> zero-shot classification capabilities. We inject the known class information from the class taxonomy defined in Cityccapes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, <span id="S10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>“road”, “building”, “wall”, “traffic light”, as these classes are commonly encountered in urban road scenes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input video frame at time <math id="S10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we initially pass it through the pre-trained SAM 2 model. We utilize a grid of equally sampled points as the segmentation prompt for SAM 2. The output of SAM 2 is a set of masks <math id="S10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of masks. For each mask <math id="S10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, we crop the content of the bounding box that encloses the segment delimitated by the mask <math id="S10.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, we classify the crop content of <math id="S10.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with CLIP, which gives a set of class probabilities <math id="S10.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of text prompts. Next, we assign the road-obstacle segmentation score for the segment <math id="S10.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as: <math id="S10.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=1-\max(P_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=1-\max(P_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we aggregate all masks along with their scores to get prediction <math id="S10.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S10.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=1-\max(P_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=1-\max(P_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We term the entire pipeline as Zero-Shot SAM2 (ZS-SAM2). ZS-SAM2 employs CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> with ViT-H trained in DFN-5B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and SAM2 architecture with the hiera large backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. From <a href="#S10.T7" title="In 10 Zero-shot Road-Obstacle Video Segmentation ‣ Road Obstacle Video Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we observe that ZS-SAM2 performs significantly poor than CC-SAM2, despite being supervised by CLIP. This indicates that while SAM2 has demonstrated impressive zero-shot performance across various computer vision tasks, it struggles with more complex scenarios such as road-obstacle video segmentation. This suggests that further modifications are necessary to adapt off-the-shelf foundation models for more complex tasks such as road-obstacle video segmentation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S10.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S10.T7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="S10.T7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between the zero-shot performance of SAM2 (ZS-SAM2) and its fine-tuned variant (CC-SAM2).</span></figcaption>
<div id="S10.T7.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:341.6pt;height:83.9pt;vertical-align:-40.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-104.9pt,25.8pt) scale(0.619436689244448,0.619436689244448) ;">
<table id="S10.T7.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S10.T7.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S10.T7.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods</span></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S10.T7.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pixel-Level Metrics</span></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S10.T7.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Component-Level Metrics</span></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S10.T7.5.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Video Metrics</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S10.T7.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S10.T7.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">AuROC<math id="S10.T7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">AuPRC<math id="S10.T7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">FPR<math id="S10.T7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{95}\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>95</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{95}\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">sIoU<math id="S10.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">PPV<math id="S10.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">F1<math id="S10.T7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}_{1}\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}_{1}\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">VC<math id="S10.T7.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S10.T7.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">Lost&amp;Found</td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.8" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.3.9" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S10.T7.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S10.T7.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"></td>
